## 1. Target format ladder (60 formats)

Wire == name for every row. `.0` = base, `.25` = M tier, `.50` = H tier.

### 1.0 The budget proof that fixes the ladder (256-weight block)

A format whose name says `B` bits per weight has a total budget of `256·B` bits for a
256-weight block. The level payload alone is `256·B` bits, so **a grouped layout at exactly
`B.00` has provably zero bits left for group state** — any per-group scale pushes the wire
over `B.00`, and sacrificing a level bit (to `B−1`) leaves a whole `1.00` BPW of state, which
is worse than the plain format at every high-BPW family (`Q_G_16` at 15-bit levels is
strictly worse than `Q_16`).

Therefore, under G-5 (wire == name), the **GRP family starts at the M tier** and the plain
base covers every integer BPW point. The GRP base tier `Q_G_x` is retired.

| Family | base (L) | M (+0.25) | H (+0.50) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Plain `Q_x` | `B.00` — B-bit levels + block FP16 (slots-funded) | `B.25` — + per-32 refinement byte | `B.50` — + per-32 FP16 group scale |
| GRP `Q_G_x` | **retired** (no room for group state) | `B.25` — + per-16 4-bit group scale codes | `B.50` — + per-16 affine (4b scale + 4b offset) |

Exact budgets (256 weights):

| Layout | Bits | BPW |
|---|---|---|
| plain base | `256·B − 16` (16 slots) + `16` (fp16) | `B.00` ✓ |
| plain M | `256·B − 16` + `16` + `64` (8 refinement bytes) | `B.25` ✓ |
| plain H | `256·B` + `8·16` (per-32 fp16 scales) | `B.50` ✓ |
| GRP M | `256·B − 16` + `16` + `64` (16 × 4-bit codes) | `B.25` ✓ |
| GRP H | `256·B − 16` + `16` + `128` (16 × 8-bit affine) | `B.50` ✓ |

The GRP H layout is byte-for-byte the GGUF **Q4_K** recipe (4-bit levels + per-group affine +
global fp16) at exactly 4.50 BPW. The plain H layout is the **Q8_0** recipe (per-32 FP16
scale) at exactly 8.50 BPW. Both are the industry-grade grouped formats, at the BPW where
industry actually puts them.

